designs.p1_retro:
With the committed papers/first-afference/run_p1.py UNMODIFIED (sha256 656eb5ad047ea44eb63fdf10af83bd0947a18f1b84363c1a55576ffc0c81736d), styxx/power_QUARANTINED.py.txt is loaded as styxx.power exactly as run_protocol_v5d._load_p1 does it. run_p1 is imported BEFORE the trace, so its `from styxx.power import PowerError, effective_n, reachable` binds the very function objects that are later swapped (asserted: run_p1.reachable is mod.reachable). The retro declares G4_refuses_degenerate: exercises [effective_n, order_stat_bar, false_positive_rate, min_detectable_bar, reachable] and traces `with cov.section('G4_refuses_degenerate'): run_p1.degenerate(np.random.default_rng(run_p1.SEED))`. On 3.10.20, 3.11.15, 3.12.3 and 3.13.12 the results are identical:
- degenerate_refusal_rate is 1.0;
- the section records exactly {'styxx.power:reachable': 12};
- score refuses [V5:NOT_EXERCISED], naming exactly effective_n, order_stat_bar, false_positive_rate and min_detectable_bar;
- all five __code__ attributes are restored after exit.
So the refusal names exactly the four unexercised functions, with no harness rewrite. It works because the swap happens at the one place every reference passes through, the function object's __code__; a binding proxy would have missed run_p1's pre-bound alias.

Nested under an outer self-trace that declares run_p1:degenerate (e6), the outer tracer credits degenerate:1 and the inner retro is still exactly {reachable: 12}. The ungated G1 companion (historical() under a declaration naming order_stat_bar) records order_stat_bar:1, reached transitively through reachable(n_draws=45). So it would not refuse, matching the transitive semantics the v5 prereg predicted.

designs.python_versions:
3.12 and 3.13 (tested on 3.12.3 and 3.13.12) use the sys.monitoring backend: local PY_START on the replicas, observation in all threads, and no setprofile. cProfile, coverage and a harness sys.setprofile all coexist. There is no FOREIGN_PROFILER refusal and no slowdown of other code.

3.10 and 3.11 (tested on 3.10.20 and 3.11.15) use the scoped per-thread setprofile backend. A fresh start is f_lasti == -1 on 3.10 and RESUME 0 on 3.11, verified against generator resumes. Behaviour specific to these versions:
- a C-level profiler refuses FOREIGN_PROFILER;
- code inside an open section runs about 10–11x slower;
- HOOK_GONE, PROFILER_REPLACED and SECTION_THREAD notes can occur;
- a user profiler dropped by CPython is restored.

3.9 is untested: no interpreter was available. It is expected to behave like 3.10 (f_lasti -1, SETUP_WITH, code.replace available since 3.8, and threading.getprofile is not needed), but it is unverified.

Semantics are identical across versions (credit = replica PY_START under a live marker before any Handle._run), with two exceptions:
- on 3.9–3.11, a section inside a generator resumed on another thread under-counts;
- the 'unattributed' diagnostic is complete only on 3.12+.

Every battery produced identical verdicts on all four tested versions. Python 3.14 needs the with-opcode set extended, because BEFORE_WITH is gone there.
